# 💿 Installation

Kickflip OS is intended to be distributed as bootable installation media.

The general installation process is designed to look something like this:

```text
Boot Kickflip OS
      │
      ▼
Welcome
      │
      ▼
Create Account
      │
      ▼
Connect to Wi-Fi
      │
      ▼
Choose Installation Drive
      │
      ▼
Confirm Installation
      │
      ▼
Install Kickflip OS
      │
      ▼
Restart
      │
      ▼
Kickflip OS Desktop
```

The installer is intended to make the installation process straightforward while still providing the important options needed before installing the system.

---

# 🧙 Kickflip OS Installer

One of the major parts of the project is the Kickflip OS installer.

The installer is designed as a graphical setup wizard.

## Welcome

The first screen introduces the user to Kickflip OS.

It can contain the Kickflip OS logo, project branding, and a short introduction.

---

## Account Setup

The account setup screen allows the user to configure their initial user account.

Typical information can include:

* Username
* Display name
* Password
* Computer name

---

## Wi-Fi Setup

The installer can provide a Wi-Fi configuration step so users can connect to a network during installation.

The installer can display available wireless networks and allow the user to select one.

---

## Drive Selection

Before installing the OS, the installer provides a drive-selection screen.

This is an important part of the installer because installing an operating system can modify or erase data on a selected drive.

The installer should clearly identify the selected drive and provide an appropriate warning before continuing.

---

## Confirm & Install

The final installation page allows the user to review their choices.

For example:

```text
Ready to Install

Username: kim
Network: Connected
Installation Drive: /dev/sda

Everything is ready.

[ Back ]     [ Install Kickflip OS ]
```

Once installation begins, the installer can copy the required operating-system files, configure the bootloader, install system configuration, and prepare the computer to boot into Kickflip OS.

---

# 💻 Live Environment

Kickflip OS can be provided as a live bootable image.

A live environment allows the operating system to start from installation media without immediately installing it to the computer's internal drive.

This makes it possible to:

* Test Kickflip OS
* View the desktop
* Test hardware
* Start the installer
* Troubleshoot problems
* Demonstrate the project
* Test the OS in a virtual machine

The live environment can serve as the starting point for the entire installation experience.

---

# 🚀 Boot Process

Kickflip OS uses a customized boot experience.

The goal is for the system to feel like Kickflip OS from the moment the computer starts.

Instead of immediately presenting a generic boot screen, the project can use custom:

* GRUB configuration
* Boot graphics
* Backgrounds
* Logos
* Boot messages
* Menu entries
* Startup branding

The boot process is one of the first opportunities to establish the identity of the operating system.

---

# 🎨 Wallpapers

Kickflip OS includes custom wallpapers designed specifically for the project.

The wallpapers are part of the visual identity of the operating system.

The project can include multiple wallpapers so that users can change the appearance of their desktop.

Possible wallpaper themes include:

* Skateboarding
* Kickflips
* Street skating
* Computer graphics
* Abstract designs
* Kickflip OS logos
* Dark desktop backgrounds
* Colorful skate-inspired artwork

The wallpaper system can continue expanding as new artwork is created.

---

# 🖥️ Desktop

The Kickflip OS desktop is intended to have its own visual identity rather than simply looking like an unmodified desktop environment.

The desktop can include:

* Custom wallpaper
* Kickflip OS branding
* Custom icons
* Application launcher
* System tray
* Network controls
* Audio controls
* Power controls
* File manager
* Terminal
* System settings

The exact desktop components may change as development continues.

---

# 🛠️ Development

Kickflip OS is a project that is expected to change frequently during development.

Things may be added, removed, redesigned, or completely replaced.

That's normal for an operating-system project.

A feature that exists today might be completely different in a future release.

The goal is not to pretend the project is finished.

The goal is to keep building.

---

# 🧪 Testing

Kickflip OS can be tested in several different environments.

## Virtual Machines

Virtual machines are useful for testing the OS without modifying a physical computer.

Possible testing environments include:

* QEMU
* VirtualBox
* VMware
* Other virtualization software

Testing inside a virtual machine makes it easier to repeatedly boot, install, break, and rebuild the operating system during development.

---

## Real Hardware

Once an image is ready, Kickflip OS can also be tested on compatible physical hardware.

Hardware testing can reveal issues that may not appear inside a virtual machine.

Examples include:

* Graphics compatibility
* Wi-Fi support
* Audio
* Keyboard input
* Trackpads
* Storage devices
* Boot firmware
* Display resolution
* Power management

---

