# 🔧 Building Kickflip OS

The build process depends on the current Kickflip OS implementation.

A typical development workflow may look like:

```bash
git clone <KICKFLIP-OS-REPOSITORY>
cd kickflip-os
```

Build the required components:

```bash
./build.sh
```

Create the bootable image:

```bash
./build-iso.sh
```

The resulting ISO can then be tested using a virtual machine or written to appropriate installation media.

> **Note:** These commands are placeholders until the project's actual build scripts and repository structure are finalized.

---

# 💾 Creating Bootable Installation Media

Once a Kickflip OS ISO has been created, it can be written to a USB drive using a suitable disk-imaging application.

A typical workflow is:

```text
Kickflip OS ISO
      │
      ▼
USB flash drive
      │
      ▼
Boot computer from USB
      │
      ▼
Kickflip OS Live Environment
      │
      ▼
Kickflip OS Installer
```

Always make sure the correct target drive is selected when writing an operating-system image.

---

# ⚠️ Important Installation Warning

Installing an operating system can modify partitions and overwrite data.

Before installing Kickflip OS:

* Back up important files.
* Make sure you have selected the correct drive.
* Understand the installation options.
* Do not install to a drive containing important data unless you intend to replace that installation.
* Test the OS in a virtual machine first when possible.

Kickflip OS is a development project, so users should expect bugs and unfinished features depending on the version.

---

# 🐛 Bugs & Issues

Kickflip OS is a work in progress.

If you find something that doesn't work correctly, document the problem as clearly as possible.

Useful information includes:

* What happened
* What you expected to happen
* What version of Kickflip OS you were using
* What hardware or virtual machine you were using
* Any error messages
* Relevant logs
* Steps required to reproduce the problem

A good bug report makes it much easier to find and fix problems.

